{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_014W1wRavA5tazP9hcdaFmqS","type":"tool_result","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Perfect! I now have all the files you requested. Let me compile a complete summary of the patterns:\n\n## Summary of Go Unix Utils Codebase Patterns\n\n### 1. **Small cmd/ Main File: cmd/yes/main.go (99 lines)**\n\nThe `yes` command is a minimal utility that repeatedly outputs a string. Key patterns:\n\n- **SIGPIPE handling**: Uses `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` at the start of main\n- **Modular functions**: Breaks logic into small, focused helpers (buildOutputLine, fillBlock, writeLoop, etc.)\n- **Block-based I/O**: Fills an 8KB buffer with repeated output to optimize performance\n- **Flag handling**: Dedicated `handleSpecialFlag()` function for --help and --version\n- **Error handling**: Checks for EPIPE errors and exits cleanly (exit 0 on SIGPIPE, exit 1 on other errors)\n\n### 2. **Test File: cmd/yes/yes_test.go (135 lines)**\n\nTesting patterns used:\n\n- **Differential testing**: Compares output against GNU `gyes` reference binary\n- **Test table pattern**: Defines test cases as a slice of structs with name, args, and expected line counts\n- **Output capture helpers**: Functions like `captureLines()` and `captureExitAndStderr()` that:\n  - Use `exec.CommandContext` with timeouts\n  - Read output with `bufio.Scanner`\n  - Close pipes to trigger SIGPIPE\n- **Parallel testing**: Tests marked with `t.Parallel()`\n- **Exit code verification**: Tests verify correct handling of SIGPIPE (expects exit code 0)\n\n### 3. **Test Utilities: pkg/testutils/difftest.go (240 lines)**\n\nThe differential testing harness provides:\n\n- **DiffTest struct**: Single test case definition with:\n  - `Name`: subtest identifier\n  - `Args`: command-line arguments\n  - `Stdin`: input data (nil means EOF immediately)\n  - `Env`: environment variable overrides (defaults to inheriting with LC_ALL=C)\n  - `WorkDir`: working directory for binary execution\n  - `ExitCode`: expected exit code (defaults to 0)\n  - `Normalize`: slice of normalization functions\n  - `ExpectedFiles`: map for verifying output files created\n\n- **RunDiffTests function**: Main entry point that:\n  - Runs each test as a named subtest\n  - Executes both Go and reference binaries with identical inputs\n  - Applies normalizers to outputs before comparing\n  - Compares stdout, stderr, and exit codes\n  - Checks expected files were created correctly\n\n- **Helper functions**:\n  - `runBinary()`: Executes a binary with timeout context\n  - `applyNormalizers()`: Applies transformations in order\n  - `compareOutputs()`: Detailed divergence reporting\n  - `TimestampNormalizer`: Built-in normalizer for timestamp patterns (for `cmd/ts` tests)\n\n### 4. **SIGPIPE Handler: pkg/sys/sigpipe.go (24 lines)**\n\nMinimal signal handling utility:\n\n- **InstallSIGPIPEHandler()**: \n  - Creates a buffered signal channel (size 1)\n  - Uses `signal.Notify()` to catch SIGPIPE (not `signal.Ignore`)\n  - Spawns a goroutine that exits the process with code 0 when SIGPIPE arrives\n  - Safe to call multiple times (each call starts a new goroutine)\n  - Matches GNU coreutils behavior\n\n### Key Patterns Summary\n\n1. **Main function structure**: Always call `sys.InstallSIGPIPEHandler()` first\n2. **Testing approach**: Differential testing against GNU reference binaries using BuildBinary to compile the Go version\n3. **Output optimization**: Use buffered I/O with block fills for repeated output\n4. **Error handling**: Distinguish between SIGPIPE (clean exit 0) and other errors (exit 1)\n5. **Test organization**: Table-driven tests with parallel execution and helper functions for output capture\n6. **Normalization**: Support pluggable normalizers for test environment differences (timestamps, locales, etc.)"}]}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7010d050-4fd5-4099-960e-f78f2e0d7bf1","uuid":"3425c7c9-2bf7-4be4-aacb-697a9bbf992e","timestamp":"2026-05-22T2