{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01LzC5jSdZ8DuTs6sNg33t9B","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So whitespace is handled specially:\n- Leading whitespace from input is preserved as-is before the output number\n- Trailing whitespace from input is preserved after the output number\n- When --from is used with leading whitespace \"  1K  \" -> \"1000  \" - leading whitespace is consumed as part of parsing, but trailing space is preserved\n\nWait, let me re-examine. With `--to=si`, `\"  1000  \"` gives `\"  1.0k  \"`. It preserves the leading and trailing whitespace. With `--from=si`, `\"  1K  \"` gives `\"1000  \"`. The leading whitespace is consumed but trailing is preserved.\n\n\nI'm noticing something odd with the rounding behavior—1149 should round to 1.1k with one decimal place, but it's showing 1.2k instead, so let me verify the actual rounding logic being applied here. I'm noticing something odd with the rounding behavior—1149 should round to 1.1k with standard nearest rounding, but it's showing 1.2k instead. This suggests GNU numfmt might be using ceiling rounding by default rather than nearest, which would explain why 1.05 becomes 1.1k and 1.149 becomes 1.2k. Let me verify this against the documentation to confirm. I'm going to test with explicit rounding options to see what GNU numfmt actually defaults to, since the behavior I'm seeing suggests it might be using \"from-zero\" rather than \"nearest\".","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":463,"cache_read_input_tokens":33245,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":463},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7010d050-4fd5-4099-960e-f78f2e0d7bf1","uuid":"cb3cf51c-bd7c-49af-a0c0-c2ac604d65fe