{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01VV2FtVYFuSyqr1nJGVqarJ","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"So the default rounding in GNU numfmt is \"from-zero\", NOT \"nearest\"!\n\n- Default: 1149->1.2k, 1050->1.1k (matches from-zero)\n- Nearest: 1149->1.1k, 1050->1.1k  \n- Up: 1149->1.2k, 1050->1.1k (matches default = from-zero)\n- Down: 1149->1.1k, 1050->1.0k\n- From-zero: 1149->1.2k, 1050->1.1k (matches default)\n\n\nThe SRD claims \"nearest\" is the default, but GNU actually uses \"from-zero\" by default, so I need to match that behavior for differential testing. Now I'm working through the exact formatting rules for --to output with base 1000, starting with values under 1000.\n\nLooking at the pattern more carefully: when I divide by a unit, if the result is less than 10, I should show one decimal place, but if it's 10 or higher, I drop the decimal entirely. Let me verify this holds across the different magnitude ranges.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1522,"cache_read_input_tokens":33708,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1522},"output_tokens":9,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7010d050-4fd5-4099-960e-f78f2e0d7bf1","uuid":"3f9b62bf-0928-415f-b2a5-1ec180706420"}
