{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01L2XDJFioXff1fuvGLgkJUi","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"OK so the pattern is:\n- If the scaled value (value / unit_base^n) is < 10, format with 1 decimal place (%.1f)\n- If the scaled value is >= 10, format as integer (%d or %.0f)\n- Numbers below the smallest unit threshold are printed as-is\n\nMore precisely:\n- If value < base (1000 for SI, 1024 for IEC): print as integer\n- Otherwise find the right suffix level, divide, and:\n  - If quotient < 10: format with 1 decimal (e.g., \"1.0k\", \"9.9k\")\n  - If quotient >= 10: format as integer (e.g., \"10k\", \"100k\", \"999k\")\n\nThe \"from-zero\" rounding rule means positive numbers round up (ceiling) and negative numbers round down (floor), away from zero. So when 9999 divided by 1000 gives 9.999, rounding from-zero produces 10.0, which then gets formatted as an integer since it's >= 10, giving \"10k\".\n\nNow I need to verify the exact behavior at the boundary—does 9950 round to \"10k\" and what about 9949?","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":924,"cache_read_input_tokens":35230,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":924},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7010d050-4fd5-4099-960e-f78f2e0d7bf1","uuid":"9290e549-8a4c-430e-965c-ad3a7fb420c8"}
