{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_01KG9ZcjxD7374iAzjGUL7LY","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"Now I understand! The whitespace handling is about padding:\n\nFor `--to=si` with `\"       1000\"`:\n- Input is 11 chars total: 7 spaces + \"1000\"\n- Output is `\"       1.0k\"` — 7 spaces + \"1.0k\" = 11 chars\n- The total width is preserved: the output is right-aligned to match input width\n\nFor `--from=si` with `\"       1K\"`:\n- Input is 9 chars: 7 spaces + \"1K\"  \n- Output is `\"     1000\"` — 5 spaces + \"1000\" = 9 chars\n\n\n- With shorter inputs like `\"  1K\"` (4 chars), the output `\"1000\"` stays at 4 chars total, maintaining the width. But when the converted number is longer than the input, like `\"1K\"` becoming `\"1000\"`, there's no padding added since the output already exceeds the original width. So the actual rule is that the output width becomes the maximum of the input width and the converted number's length. I'm verifying the alignment logic by working through a few examples. When converting between formats, the number gets right-aligned within its original field width while preserving any trailing whitespace separately. Let me confirm this holds for the reverse conversion too—converting \"1000\" back to SI notation should produce \"1.0k\" right-aligned in the same 6-character field, giving \"  1.0k\" plus the trailing spaces.\n\nThis auto-padding behavior is actually the default in gnumfmt, not something that requires an explicit flag. I need to update the `processLine` function to implement this correctly.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1273,"cache_read_input_tokens":52482,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1273},"output_tokens":12,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management