{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-4-6","id":"msg_013Y6qqgsVCKAwvmVfkWn7oo","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"The problem is that my `processLine` is now auto-padding based on the input width. But for numbers like `10000`, the input is 5 chars and the output is `10k` (3 chars), so it pads to 5. But gnumfmt doesn't pad for simple numbers without leading whitespace.\n\nSo the auto-padding should only apply when there's leading whitespace. If there's no leading whitespace, no padding is needed.\n\nLet me check: the input \"10000\" has no leading whitespace, so fieldWidth = 5, but the output should just be \"10k\" without any padding.\n\nThe rule seems to be: only pad if there's leading whitespace in the input. \n\nI think the key insight is that padding should only extend to the width of the leading whitespace plus the number itself, not the entire field width including trailing whitespace. So for `\"  1000  \"`, I'd pad the output to 6 chars (2 leading + 4 for the number), giving `\"  1.0k\"`, but for `\"10000\"` with no leading whitespace, there's no padding needed at all.\n\nThe pattern seems to be that GNU only applies auto-padding when there's already leading whitespace in the input—it's preserving the alignment structure rather than creating new padding where none existed.","signature":""}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":1,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1083,"cache_read_input_tokens":55498,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1083},"output_tokens":8,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"not_available"},"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"7010d050-4fd5-4099-960e-f78f2e0d7bf1","uuid":"7010a65f-33b2-45a7-96ae-a2a360cf464a"}
